      "section": "introduction",
      "text": "per volume of water treated) are often required to meet stringent treatment goals, raising treatment costs and concerns about environ - mental impacts associated with frequent media replacement (Liu et al., 2022). More recently, anion exchange resins (AERs) have emerged as a cost- competitive alternative adsorbent media for PFAS removal (Moeini et al., 2022; Franke et al., 2021). High affinity for PFAS adsorption to the polymeric resins is derived from a combination of electrostatic and hy - drophobic interactions (Fang et al., 2021). In addition to resins that can be regenerated upon PFAS breakthrough (when PFASs are detected in effluent samples) using a combination of salt brine and alcohol cosol - vent, manufacturers have recently introduced PFAS-selective AERs that are applied as single-use media, which are disposed or incinerated after contaminant breakthrough. A growing body of literature has docu - mented the superiority of AERs over GAC adsorbents in treating PFAS-impacted groundwater (McCleaf et al., 2017; Murray et al., 2021; Boyer et al., 2021a). In particular, field studies have consistently shown that AERs can treat a much greater volume of water before observing breakthrough of most PFASs (Woodard et al., 2017; Zeng et al., 2020;"
